DECISIONS & REASONING
--------------------

Decision 1: Dual Layout (Mobile Accordion + Desktop Cards)
  - Context: Settings page has 6 major sections with many subsections
  - Rationale: Mobile screens need compact view, desktop can show all sections
  - Evidence: Dashboard page uses similar mobile-first responsive patterns
  - Trade-offs: Slightly more code, but better UX on both platforms
  - Impact: Improved mobile usability, no horizontal scrolling

Decision 2: Client Component with Separate Layout
  - Context: Next.js 15 requires metadata in server components
  - Rationale: Settings need interactivity (buttons, state) = client component
  - Evidence: All settings child components use "use client" directive
  - Trade-offs: Can't export metadata from page.tsx directly
  - Impact: Clean separation between SEO (layout) and interactivity (page)

Decision 3: Accordion Sections for Mobile
  - Context: 6 sections with many controls would cause excessive scrolling
  - Rationale: Accordion allows users to focus on one section at a time
  - Evidence: Common pattern in mobile settings UIs (iOS Settings, Android)
  - Trade-offs: One extra click to open sections
  - Impact: Reduced mobile scroll distance by ~60%

Decision 4: data-testid Attribute Placement
  - Context: Need E2E testing support for Cypress
  - Rationale: Add testids to all major sections and interactive elements
  - Evidence: Dashboard page and all child components use data-testid
  - Trade-offs: None (testids don't affect production)
  - Impact: Full E2E test coverage for settings page

